/rust/target
/target
# portal-client-ffi is a standalone workspace (like isekai-client-ffi and
# portal-server/portal-client before it), not a member of rust/'s workspace,
# so /rust/target above doesn't cover its build output.
/rust/portal-client-ffi/target

# Private keys any of these tools writes with `load_or_generate_key` or
# `endpoint_cert::load_or_generate_cert_key`. Long-lived secrets — never commit.
#
# **Listed by shape as well as by name, deliberately.** This block used to be
# names only, and every new tool that wrote a key added one more line after
# somebody noticed — `*-video-tls.pem` still carries the comment saying
# `*-endpoint.pem` did not cover it. `portal-server.pem` was not covered by any
# of them and reached a pull request.
*.pem
# The one PEM that is meant to be here: a public CA bundle, no key in it.
# **One line per app.** The rule above is by shape, so each app that ships the
# bundle needs its own exception; android-portal's was missing, and the app
# reached main unable to complete a single TLS handshake -- quictls had no
# roots, so every connection died as "connection lost" before pairing could
# even be attempted.
!android/app/src/main/assets/cacert.pem
!android-portal/app/src/main/assets/cacert.pem
#
# **Nobody has written down where the bundle came from or as of when.** Both
# copies are byte-identical (sha256 6d84ab71cb72...); the first arrived with
# the Android camera client in #133 on 2026-08-18, with no source recorded.
# As of 2026-10-14 it holds 146 roots, two of them already past notAfter
# (SECOM Security Communication RootCA1, 2023-09-30; Baltimore CyberTrust
# Root, 2025-05-12) -- harmless, since an expired root is simply not trusted,
# but there is no signal for when the store itself needs refreshing. A stale
# trust store fails exactly as opaquely as the missing file did: "connection
# lost", with nothing naming the certificate.

# Auth0 tokens the camera apps store after a device sign-in. The refresh token
# in here mints access tokens for the signed-in account until it is revoked, so
# it is the same kind of secret as the key above and is written the same way
# (0600, beside the key). Never commit.
*-auth0.json

# The service catalogue an operator writes for their own machine. `--config`
# names it and `portal-server --example-config` prints a starter; what ends up
# in it is local addresses and which of them this host offers. It was committed
# once by accident and removed again (#200), and has been swept into a `git add`
# several times since — ignoring it is cheaper than noticing each time.
/rust/portal-server.toml

# The gateway policy, for the same reason and by the same route:
# `--gateway-config` names it and `portal-server --example-gateway-config`
# prints a starter, which `docs/portal.md` says to redirect into this very
# path. It carries a deployment's operations and attribute ranges. This one
# was swept in by a `git add -A` and removed again.
/rust/gateway.toml

# cargo-mutants writes its report here, and keeps the previous run beside it.
/rust/mutants.out/
/rust/mutants.out.old/

# And a mutant that makes `config_dir` answer with a relative path writes the
# consent record into the crate directory instead of the user's. The test that
# catches that mutant runs after it, so the file is left behind either way.
/rust/*/*-privacy-consent.json

# iOS build outputs. `ios/build-rust.sh` writes the bindings and the
# xcframework; `xcodegen generate` writes the project and its Info.plist. Only
# ios/project.yml and ios/IsekaiCameraClient/App are tracked.
/ios/IsekaiCameraClient.xcodeproj/
/ios/IsekaiCameraClient/Generated/
/ios/IsekaiCameraClient/Frameworks/
/ios/IsekaiCameraClient/Info.plist
/ios/DerivedData/

# QUIC trace artifacts written by msquic (ETW logging) when running the camera
# apps. Generated on demand; never committed.
quic.etl
quic.txt

# Release bundles built by `scripts/bundle-apps.sh` (and by the
# camera-apps workflow, which runs it). Hundreds of megabytes of copied
# libraries; regenerated from a build.
/dist

# Python bytecode. `.github/scripts/` is the first Python in this tree, and
# importing one of those scripts -- which is how you test it rather than just
# run it -- leaves a `__pycache__` beside it.
__pycache__/
